			<div class="content" id="post-content-4813851">
									

<p><strong>Seeking Greenlights:</strong> Yes</p>
<p><strong>Page Type:</strong> SCP Article</p>
<p><strong>Genre:</strong> Drama/Emotional</p>
<p><strong>Page Layout:</strong> A standard SCP article.</p>
<p><strong>Elevator Pitch:</strong> SCP-XXXX must always be looking into a reflective surface. Be it a mirror, or glass, or water, she must never look at anything but a reflective surface. If her direct or paracentral vision ever looks at or is blocked by an opaque surface, a reflection desynchronisation will occur in any reflective surface in any given location in the world (designated as SCP-XXXX-1). The location of SCP-XXXX-1 appears random but the time frame and range in which SCP-XXXX-1 occur seem to increase with every breach in SCP-XXXX's protocol.</p>
<div class="collapsible-block">
<div class="collapsible-block-folded"><a class="collapsible-block-link" href="javascript:;">+&nbsp;show&nbsp;block</a></div>
<div class="collapsible-block-unfolded" style="display:none">
<div class="collapsible-block-unfolded-link"><a class="collapsible-block-link" href="javascript:;">–&nbsp;hide&nbsp;block</a></div>
<div class="collapsible-block-content">
<p>SCP-XXXX doesn't appear to age and has numerous scars across her body, with the deepest around her eyes. Her inner eyelids appear to be coated in a flexible, reflective material. The reflective surfaces SCP-XXXX look into also go through a process of &quot;decay&quot;, meaning they start to dull and crack over time, including the ones in her eyes. Researchers who look into the same surface as SCP-XXXX claim they feel a sense of shame, guilt or worthlessness when they cast a reflection. As if something is judging them.</p>
<p><strong>Central Narrative:</strong> SCP-XXXX's story unfolds through a number of interviews with SCP-XXXX-1's victims. During interviews, the victims describe the reflections screaming vitriol and abuse of a deeply personal nature. Throughout the interviews, many of the victim's comment that they find SCP-XXXX-1's judgements/insults both violating (insults SCP-XXXX-1 use suggest a definite breach of privacy) and confusing (as the believed no one could have witnessed the actions SCP-XXXX-1 speaks of). Following incidences with SCP-XXXX-1, the victim's become very uncomfortable around reflective surfaces, even after amnestics are administered.</p>
<p>Also, after further research connected to her discovery, SCP-XXXX's head researcher decides to interview SCP-XXXX directly about her condition and why she causes SCP-XXXX-1. SCP-XXXX claims she doesn't know, only that before she was taken in by the Foundation, her captives mutilated her regularly to make her immortal. When asked why, SCP-XXXX commented, &quot;They said they had to. To stop them from rebelling&quot;. This leads to researchers to believe that SCP-XXXX's condition isn't in reality to cause SCP-XXXX-1 but to prevent it.</p>
<p><strong>Hook/Attention-Grabber:</strong> After interviews with SCP-XXXX and observations in her character, Foundation researchers begin to believe that SCP-XXXX may be in fact Elaine of Astolat, also known as The Lady of Shallot from Alfred Tennyson famous poem. Her conditions aren't dissimilar to the women described in the poem. This supposed curse Tennyson describes may be a misinterpretation of SCP-XXXX's condition.</p>
</div>
</div>
