<p>Thanks for replying.</p>
<blockquote>
<p>I'm moderately concerned by your reliance in Series I articles to carry some key details of the article. Crosslinking is alright, but crosslinking to S1 in this way is a tad strange.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>They don't need to be there. I just used them to emphasis the effects SCP-XXXX has on the mirrors. It isn't necessary.</p>
<blockquote>
<p>Additionally, I've got some issues with the idea of the people in the mirrors — it comes off as comical more than anything? Like, my reflection insulting me and demanding to be free doesn't spark feelings of fear or dread, just mild bemusement.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>I think I wasn't describing what I wanted correctly (not used to this). When I mean abusive, I wasn't imagining like &quot;You're smelly and I hate you&quot; or &quot;You have no friends and nobody likes you&quot;. That's teenage, self-loathing movie stuff. I was picturing very deep and incredibly personal insults. Insults that come from very private things.</p>
<p>Think of it like this. How many reflections are around you right now? Not just mirrors, but windows, glass, metal, your phone, pictures frames, anything. Reflections are all around us, mirroring us all the time. That means they see everything. Think about what they're reflecting and what you're doing when they're reflecting you. They not only witness you washing your face and applying make up. They witness you degrade people online. They witness you killing people. They witness you doing vile, disgusting, shameful, perverted, pitiful things. And they have to mirror that.</p>
<p>Don't forget, what makes this worse is the reflections are always like this. Even after they've resynchronised. No matter what you do, someone is going to be watching. Judging.</p>

							</div>

								
					
