			<div class="content" id="post-content-4824603">
									

<p>You may want to look at my answer to JamaisJ to get more of information.</p>
<blockquote>
<p>But the whole setup of interviewing the anomaly and then she drops some important information (amidst a backstory about shadowy kidnappers) seems constructed, and more importantly, lacking in conflict. There aren't really stakes involved here that make this reveal dramatic.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>I would have added this in but I wasn't sure how important the discovery was. My idea as that SCP-XXXX was discovered in Farringford House in the 40s during its hand over to the British Holiday Estates Ltd. Upon SCP-XXXX's discovery, the first recorded SCP-XXXX-1 occurred. SCP-XXXX was later taken in by the Foundation for research and containment. During the interview, we learn more about these mutilations and scars and discover these 'kidnappers' weren't kidnappers in reality. They were her guards. They were essentially doing what the foundation is currently doing. Keeping SCP-XXXX out of sight and ensuring SCP-XXXX-1 didn't happen.</p>
<div class="collapsible-block">
<div class="collapsible-block-folded"><a class="collapsible-block-link" href="javascript:;">+&nbsp;show&nbsp;block</a></div>
<div class="collapsible-block-unfolded" style="display:none">
<div class="collapsible-block-unfolded-link"><a class="collapsible-block-link" href="javascript:;">–&nbsp;hide&nbsp;block</a></div>
<div class="collapsible-block-content">
<blockquote>
<p>I encourage you to also implement a more standard, uh, rising-action/climax format as well.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>What I'm planning in the climax is made the dramatic ending you'd like. The coating on SCP-XXXX's eyelids is also decaying, all be it slower than regular reflective surfaces but still at a measurable rate. This means that at some point, SCP-XXXX's coating will stop working. Then the problems for the Foundation begin. How will they deal with her need for blinking or sleeping? Blinking the could manage but sleeping would be harder. She may not age but she can still die of other means. Really, the moment her eyelid coating ceases working, the likelihood of SCP-XXXX-1 occurring increases.</p>
<blockquote>
<p>I meant to bring this up, but how involved is this reference going to be? It seems like a throwaway atm, with no purpose but gesturing towards a popular poem as an inspiration. Is there some story going on about her being Tennyson's inspiration?</p>
</blockquote>
<p>This ties into her discover. As she was discovered at Farringford House, there is a good chance she was connected to Tennyson as the Tennyson family owned Farringford House until the 40s. My idea is that Tennyson and his Pre-Raphaelite Brotherhood (real thing btw) may have either be involved with SCP-XXXX's creation or were just her caretakers. Also, SCP-XXXX's imprisonment and abilities may have taken its toll on the Brotherhood. SCP-XXXX could be why Tennyson's brother, Edward Tennyson ended up in a private institution. Tennyson wrote The Lady of Shallot as a means of dealing with all the horrible things they do to SCP-XXXX. The poem just acts as further evidence of her involvement with Tennyson. Now that you point it out, however, I do think that the poem is more of an additional note than an actual hook. The hook is more likely her decaying eyelid coating and the fact that the SCP-XXXX's situation and containment may get worse and harder to control.</p>
</div>
