<p>Excellent, excellent; thank you for clarifying. This is shaping up real well.</p>
<p>Responding to a conversation that occurred in PMs, I still believe the &quot;middle&quot; of the story you've got is a little lacking. Ideally you would want to fill it with primary evidence taken from the premises of her previous holders, such as a convenient journal entry (either documenting someone's personal thoughts or, more ideally, &quot;containment procedures&quot; and incident reports). This could be followed by a Foundation incident report of some kind, which would highlight the malevolent intent of the reflections.</p>
<p>I also still take issue with this end-of-article twist being revealed in an interview room, given upon being asked for it nicely. There should be something in the way of the audience learning this information that, when it's overcome, is satisfying. I'd imagine this would be as simple as having her be non-responsive to questioning, and then reveals the information to a researcher after some big event occurs (that aforementioned incident report, perhaps?). I'm imagining a scene of the girl and a researcher standing in front of a mirror in her containment chamber, when she suddenly begins to speak to them — the imagery, at least, is more varied than a mundane interview room.</p>
<p>This execution would require you to really nail the feeling of dread that should be emanating from these reflection people, to make them seem like a good thread. Does any of that sound interesting?</p>

